//! member: false
//!
//! ls — list directory contents.
//!
//! References consulted: POSIX.1-2024 `ls`; GNU coreutils 9.11
//! `ls --help`, the info manual's `ls invocation` node and its
//! sub-nodes, and black-box runs of the GNU binary (the staged 9.11 on
//! linux x86-64, Homebrew's `gls` 9.11 on macOS arm64) under
//! `LC_ALL=C`; uutils' and toybox's `ls` as design references only.
//! Never GNU source. The probes behind every rule below are kasumi
//! `~/lanes/bu18/probe*.sh`, `~/lanes/bu20/probe*.sh`, `notes/bu18-ls.md`
//! and `notes/bu20-the-long-format.md`.
//!
//! **Into a pipe or a file**, where scripts read it: one name per line
//! unless `-C`, `-x`, `-m` or `-l` asks, names printed as they are
//! unless `-q` asks.
//!
//! **On a terminal** (`os_isatty(1)`, wolf 0.2.26, s215; bu19), GNU's
//! terminal defaults, measured through a pseudo-terminal against 9.11:
//! columns unless a format was asked for; the shell-escape quoting style
//! (`'with space'`, `'new'$'\n''line'`, `''$'\303\251'`), with one
//! leading space before every unquoted name of a `-C`/`-x` listing that
//! holds a quoted one, and quoted directory headers; and under `-N`
//! (`--literal`) `?` for an unprintable byte unless
//! `--show-control-chars`. The width is `-w`, then `COLUMNS`, then 80:
//! GNU asks the terminal its own width before `COLUMNS`, and wolf cannot
//! (wolf-lang#643), so the two agree on a terminal 80 wide or one that
//! reports no size.
//!
//! GNU's rules, each measured against 9.11:
//!
//! - Operands: none means `.`. Files first, as one listing; then each
//!   directory, under a `NAME:` header when there was more than one
//!   operand (a failed one counts) or `-R`, a blank line before every
//!   header that follows output. `-d` lists the operands themselves.
//! - The last of `-1 -C -x -m -l` (and `--format`) wins, except that
//!   `-1` after `-l` leaves the long format; so does the last of
//!   `-a`/`-A`, and of `-S`/`-t`/`-X`/`-U`/`--sort`.
//! - Sorting is by byte (the C locale); `-S` is largest first, `-t`
//!   newest first, `-X` by the text after the last `.`; every tie falls
//!   to the name, and `-r` reverses the whole order. `-U` and
//!   `--sort=none` keep the directory's own order
//!   (`fs_read_dir_entries`, wolf 0.2.27), and `-f` is `-a -U`.
//!   `--group-directories-first` puts directories (a link to one
//!   included) first, and `-r` does not move them.
//! - **The long format** (`-l`, and `-g`, `-n`, `-o`, `--full-time`,
//!   which imply it): the mode's ten characters, the link count, the
//!   owner and the group (`-g` drops the owner, `-o` and `-G` the group,
//!   `-n` prints both as numbers), the size — a device file's major and
//!   minor numbers instead — the time, the name, and ` -> TARGET` after
//!   a link. Every column is as wide as its widest entry IN THAT
//!   LISTING: numbers to the right, names to the left. A listing of a
//!   directory starts with `total N`, the blocks its entries hold.
//! - **The time** is the modification time, or with `-u`/`--time=atime`
//!   the access time, `-c`/`--time=ctime` the status change,
//!   `--time=birth` the creation. In the long format it prints as
//!   `Sep 13 12:26` when it is within the last six months (15,778,476
//!   seconds) and not in the future, else `Sep 13  2020`;
//!   `--full-time` and `--time-style` (and `TIME_STYLE`) choose the ISO
//!   forms. A time option WITHOUT the long format sorts by that time,
//!   unless a sort was asked for. Times are local: `bore/clock.lu`
//!   reads the zone `TZ` names.
//! - `-s` puts each entry's allocated blocks before it and `-i` its
//!   inode number, in every format, right-aligned in the listing's
//!   widest. Blocks are 1024 bytes (512 under `POSIXLY_CORRECT`, unless
//!   `-k`); `-h` and `--si` print sizes and blocks in units, rounded up.
//! - `-F` appends `/` to a directory, `@` to a link, `*` to a regular
//!   file with an execute bit, `|` to a pipe and `=` to a socket;
//!   `--file-type` the same less the `*`; `-p` the `/` only. In the long
//!   format the link's NAME is unmarked and, under `-F` and
//!   `--file-type`, its target carries the mark of what it resolves to.
//!   `-q` prints every byte outside printable ASCII as `?`.
//! - **Links.** A link is itself, never its target, except: a
//!   command-line link to a directory is followed (listed as the
//!   directory) unless `-l`, `-d` or `-F` asked; `-H` follows every
//!   command-line link; `-L` follows every link, and a link it cannot
//!   follow is a diagnostic, status 1, and a row of `?`. `-R` under
//!   `-L` does not list a directory it is already inside (same device
//!   and inode), and says so, status 2.
//! - Statuses: 2 for an operand that cannot be reached or a directory
//!   operand that cannot be opened, and for a usage error; 1 for an
//!   entry that cannot be read, a directory `-R` meets and cannot open,
//!   and for a `--sort`/`--time`/`--format` word GNU's argmatch
//!   refuses; otherwise 0.
//!
//! **What wolf cannot see, and what that costs** (each a stated skip in
//! `tests/cases/ls.toml`, or a refusal by name, never an imitation):
//!
//! - **Owner and group NAMES** come from `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/group`
//!   (`bore/ids.lu`); an account kept anywhere else (LDAP, systemd's
//!   dynamic users, macOS's directory service) prints as its number.
//! - **The `+` and `.` after a mode**, GNU's marks for an ACL and a
//!   security context: wolf reads no extended attribute
//!   (wolf-lang#685), so neither is ever printed.
//! - **A name that is not UTF-8** is listed by the directory but cannot
//!   be named to any path call (ruling #60: a byte-path family is a
//!   later lane), and this program holds names as `str`: such a name is
//!   reported and skipped, status 1, where bu18 failed the whole
//!   directory.
//! - `-v`, `--sort=width`, the quoting styles but `-N` (`-b -Q
//!   --quoting-style`, and the `QUOTING_STYLE` variable is not read),
//!   `-I --ignore --hide -B`, `-D`, `-Z`, `--author`,
//!   `--color=always`, `--hyperlink`, `--zero`, `--block-size` (and the
//!   `LS_BLOCK_SIZE`, `BLOCK_SIZE` and `BLOCKSIZE` variables are not
//!   read) and `--time-style=+FORMAT` are not written yet, and are
//!   refused by name (status 2) rather than ignored.

use bore

/// What the command line asked for.
struct Opts {
    all: int,
    directory: bool,
    recursive: bool,
    format: int,
    width: int,
    width_given: bool,
    tab: int,
    sort: int,
    sort_given: bool,
    time_given: bool,
    time_kind: int,
    time_style: int,
    style_word: str,
    reverse: bool,
    indicator: int,
    hide: bool,
    hide_given: bool,
    quote: bool,
    literal: bool,
    format_given: bool,
    deref: int,
    deref_given: bool,
    group_dirs: bool,
    blocks: bool,
    inode: bool,
    owner: bool,
    group: bool,
    numeric: bool,
    human: int,
    kibi: bool,
    refused: str,
}

/// What a long listing needs beside the entries: the account tables,
/// the time zone, and the instant the six-month rule is measured from.
/// Read once, before any listing's region.
struct Ctx {
    names: bore.Names,
    zone: bore.Zone,
    now: int,
    now_nanos: int,
    posix: bool,
}

/// One name to list: what it is called, the directory it was listed
/// in (`""` for an operand, whose name is its path), and what the
/// directory and a stat said. The path is built only when a stat or a
/// diagnostic needs it, so a plain listing allocates none.
///
/// `kind` is `[os.fs.stat]`'s code, -1 while nothing has said; `typed`
/// says it can be trusted for a mark (a directory entry's own type, or
/// a stat). `ok` says `rec` holds twenty words. For a link read for the
/// long format, `target` is where it points, and `to_kind`/`to_mode`
/// what that resolves to (-1: nothing, or never asked).
struct Entry {
    name: str,
    dir: str,
    known: bool,
    ok: bool,
    typed: bool,
    kind: int,
    rec: List[int],
    target: str,
    arrow: bool,
    to_kind: int,
    to_mode: int,
    code: int,
}

/// An entry not stat'ed yet, of a kind the directory gave (or -1).
fn entry(name: str, dir: str, kind: int) -> Entry {
    Entry {
        name: name,
        dir: dir,
        known: false,
        ok: false,
        typed: kind >= 0 && kind != 2,
        kind: kind,
        rec: List[int](),
        target: "",
        arrow: false,
        to_kind: -1,
        to_mode: 0,
        code: 0,
    }
}

/// The path that reaches `e`.
fn path_of(e: Entry) -> str {
    if e.dir.len == 0 {
        return e.name
    }
    joined(e.dir, e.name)
}

// The words for `format`, `sort`, `indicator`, `deref` and the times.
fn one_per_line() -> int {
    0
}

fn vertical() -> int {
    1
}

fn across() -> int {
    2
}

fn with_commas() -> int {
    3
}

fn long_format() -> int {
    4
}

fn by_name() -> int {
    0
}

fn by_size() -> int {
    1
}

fn by_time() -> int {
    2
}

fn by_extension() -> int {
    3
}

fn unsorted() -> int {
    4
}

fn by_version() -> int {
    5
}

fn by_width() -> int {
    6
}

fn no_indicator() -> int {
    0
}

fn slash() -> int {
    1
}

fn classify() -> int {
    2
}

fn file_type() -> int {
    3
}

fn follow_dir_operands() -> int {
    0
}

fn follow_operands() -> int {
    1
}

fn follow_always() -> int {
    2
}

fn follow_never() -> int {
    3
}

/// `--time-style`: the C locale's two forms, or an ISO one.
fn style_locale() -> int {
    0
}

fn style_long_iso() -> int {
    1
}

fn style_iso() -> int {
    2
}

fn style_full_iso() -> int {
    3
}

/// Six months, as GNU counts them: half a mean Gregorian year.
fn six_months() -> int {
    15778476
}

fn main() -> int {
    let p = bore.parse(options(), env_args(), bore.permute())
    var out = bore.stdout()
    var o = Opts {
        all: 0,
        directory: false,
        recursive: false,
        format: one_per_line(),
        width: 80,
        width_given: false,
        tab: 8,
        sort: by_name(),
        sort_given: false,
        time_given: false,
        time_kind: 0,
        time_style: -1,
        style_word: "",
        reverse: false,
        indicator: no_indicator(),
        hide: false,
        hide_given: false,
        quote: false,
        literal: false,
        format_given: false,
        deref: follow_dir_operands(),
        deref_given: false,
        group_dirs: false,
        blocks: false,
        inode: false,
        owner: true,
        group: true,
        numeric: false,
        human: 0,
        kibi: false,
        refused: "",
    }
    var i = 0
    while i < p.keys.len {
        let k = p.keys[i]
        let v = p.values[i]
        if k == "help" {
            bore.put(mut out, help()) else |_| {}
            return done(mut out, 0)
        }
        if k == "version" {
            bore.put(mut out, bore.version_text("ls")) else |_| {}
            return done(mut out, 0)
        }
        let status = take_option(mut o, k, v, p.given[i])
        if status != 0 {
            return status
        }
        i += 1
    }
    if !bore.ok(p) {
        eprint("ls: {p.error}\nTry 'ls --help' for more information.")
        return 2
    }
    if o.sort == by_version() && o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "-v and --sort=version are not written yet"
    }
    if o.sort == by_width() && o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "--sort=width is not written yet"
    }
    if o.refused.len > 0 {
        bore.warn("ls", o.refused)
        return 2
    }
    // Measured: a time option sorts by that time only where it has no
    // column to show it in.
    if o.time_given && !o.sort_given && o.format != long_format() {
        o.sort = by_time()
    }
    // `TIME_STYLE` stands in for a `--time-style` nobody gave; a word in
    // it GNU would refuse is refused the same way.
    if o.time_style < 0 {
        var asked = o.time_style == -2
        if !asked && is_set("TIME_STYLE") {
            // Measured: set and empty is a word like any other, and
            // ambiguous.
            asked = true
            o.style_word = env_get("TIME_STYLE") else ""
        }
        o.time_style = style_locale()
        if o.format == long_format() && asked {
            let status = take_time_style(mut o, o.style_word)
            if status != 0 {
                return status
            }
            if o.refused.len > 0 {
                bore.warn("ls", o.refused)
                return 2
            }
        }
    }
    // A command-line link to a directory is followed unless the listing
    // is about the link itself (measured: -l, -d and -F proper; -p and
    // --file-type still follow).
    if !o.deref_given {
        if o.format == long_format() || o.directory || o.indicator == classify() {
            o.deref = follow_never()
        }
    }
    // On a terminal GNU's defaults change (measured through a
    // pseudo-terminal, 9.11): columns, `?` for an unprintable byte, and
    // the shell-escape quoting style, unless the command line chose. A
    // closed descriptor 1 is not a terminal.
    let tty = os_isatty(1) else false
    if tty {
        if !o.format_given {
            o.format = vertical()
        }
        if !o.hide_given {
            o.hide = true
        }
        if !o.literal {
            o.quote = true
        }
    }
    if o.format == vertical() || o.format == across() || o.format == with_commas() {
        if !o.width_given {
            o.width = columns()
        }
    }
    // The tables and the zone a long listing reads, once, out here:
    // every listing below runs in a region of its own.
    var cx = Ctx {
        names: bore.no_names(),
        zone: bore.utc(),
        now: 0,
        now_nanos: 0,
        posix: false,
    }
    if o.format == long_format() {
        if !o.numeric {
            cx.names = bore.read_names()
        }
        cx.zone = bore.zone()
        let ms = time_unix_ms()
        cx.now = ms / 1000
        // The clock answers in milliseconds, and a file made in this
        // one must not read as made in the future: the end of it.
        cx.now_nanos = ms % 1000 * 1000000 + 999999
    }
    cx.posix = !bore.permute()
    var operands = copy p.operands
    if operands.len == 0 {
        (mut operands).push(".")
    }
    var failed = false
    let status = list_operands(mut out, o, cx, operands, mut failed)
    if failed {
        bore.write_error(out, "ls", "write error")
        return 2
    }
    done(mut out, status)
}

/// `bore.finish`, but a failed write is status 2: measured for `ls >&-`
/// and `ls > /dev/full`, where the utilities `bore.finish` was measured
/// on say 1.
fn done(mut out: bore.Out, status: int) -> int {
    bore.flush(mut out) else |_| {
        bore.write_error(out, "ls", "write error")
        return 2
    }
    status
}

/// Apply one option, in command-line order, so the last of a set wins
/// as it does in GNU's. Answers a status to exit with at once (a word
/// GNU refuses there and then), or 0.
fn take_option(mut o: Opts, k: str, v: str, given: bool) -> int {
    if k == "all" {
        o.all = 2
    } else if k == "almost-all" {
        o.all = 1
    } else if k == "directory" {
        o.directory = true
    } else if k == "recursive" {
        o.recursive = true
    } else if k == "1" {
        // Measured: `-l -1` stays long, where `--format=single-column`
        // does not.
        if o.format != long_format() {
            o.format = one_per_line()
        }
        o.format_given = true
    } else if k == "C" {
        o.format = vertical()
        o.format_given = true
    } else if k == "x" {
        o.format = across()
        o.format_given = true
    } else if k == "m" {
        o.format = with_commas()
        o.format_given = true
    } else if k == "l" {
        o.format = long_format()
        o.format_given = true
    } else if k == "g" {
        o.format = long_format()
        o.format_given = true
        o.owner = false
    } else if k == "o" {
        o.format = long_format()
        o.format_given = true
        o.group = false
    } else if k == "numeric-uid-gid" {
        o.format = long_format()
        o.format_given = true
        o.numeric = true
    } else if k == "full-time" {
        o.format = long_format()
        o.format_given = true
        o.time_style = style_full_iso()
    } else if k == "no-group" {
        o.group = false
    } else if k == "time-style" {
        // Measured: the word is read only when a long listing is
        // printed, so `ls --time-style=bogus` lists and exits 0.
        o.style_word = v
        o.time_style = -2
    } else if k == "literal" {
        o.literal = true
    } else if k == "format" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, format_words(), format_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error(
                "ls",
                v,
                "format",
                format_words(),
                format_meanings(),
                m == -2,
            )
        }
        o.format = m
        o.format_given = true
    } else if k == "S" {
        o.sort = by_size()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "t" {
        o.sort = by_time()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "X" {
        o.sort = by_extension()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "U" {
        o.sort = unsorted()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "f" {
        o.sort = unsorted()
        o.sort_given = true
        o.all = 2
    } else if k == "v" {
        o.sort = by_version()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "sort" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, sort_words(), sort_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error("ls", v, "sort", sort_words(), sort_meanings(), m == -2)
        }
        o.sort = m
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "time" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, time_words(), time_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error("ls", v, "time", time_words(), time_meanings(), m == -2)
        }
        o.time_kind = m
        o.time_given = true
    } else if k == "u" {
        o.time_kind = 1
        o.time_given = true
    } else if k == "c" {
        o.time_kind = 2
        o.time_given = true
    } else if k == "reverse" {
        o.reverse = true
    } else if k == "F" {
        o.indicator = classify()
    } else if k == "p" {
        o.indicator = slash()
    } else if k == "file-type" {
        o.indicator = file_type()
    } else if k == "classify" {
        if !given {
            o.indicator = classify()
        } else {
            let m = bore.argmatch(v, when_words(), when_meanings())
            if m < 0 {
                return bore.argmatch_error(
                    "ls",
                    v,
                    "classify",
                    when_words(),
                    when_meanings(),
                    m == -2,
                )
            }
            // Measured: `--classify=never` and `=auto` (off a terminal)
            // change nothing, not even an earlier -F or -p.
            if m == 0 {
                o.indicator = classify()
            }
        }
    } else if k == "indicator-style" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, indicator_words(), indicator_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error(
                "ls",
                v,
                "indicator-style",
                indicator_words(),
                indicator_meanings(),
                m == -2,
            )
        }
        o.indicator = m
    } else if k == "hide-control-chars" {
        o.hide = true
        o.hide_given = true
    } else if k == "show-control-chars" {
        o.hide = false
        o.hide_given = true
    } else if k == "dereference-command-line" {
        o.deref = follow_operands()
        o.deref_given = true
    } else if k == "dereference" {
        o.deref = follow_always()
        o.deref_given = true
    } else if k == "dereference-command-line-symlink-to-dir" {
        o.deref = follow_dir_operands()
        o.deref_given = true
    } else if k == "group-directories-first" {
        o.group_dirs = true
    } else if k == "color" {
        if !given {
            o.refused = "--color=always is not written yet"
        } else {
            let m = bore.argmatch(v, when_words(), when_meanings())
            if m < 0 {
                return bore.argmatch_error("ls", v, "color", when_words(), when_meanings(), m == -2)
            }
            if m == 0 {
                o.refused = "--color=always is not written yet"
            }
        }
    } else if k == "width" {
        let w = line_width(v)
        if w < 0 {
            bore.warn("ls", "invalid line width: {bore.quote(v)}")
            return 2
        }
        o.width = w
        o.width_given = true
    } else if k == "tabsize" {
        let t = line_width(v)
        if t < 0 {
            bore.warn("ls", "invalid tab size: {bore.quote(v)}")
            return 2
        }
        o.tab = t
    } else if k == "inode" {
        o.inode = true
    } else if k == "size" {
        o.blocks = true
    } else if k == "human-readable" {
        o.human = 1
    } else if k == "si" {
        o.human = 2
    } else if k == "kibibytes" {
        o.kibi = true
    } else if o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "option '{shown_option(k)}' is not written yet"
    }
    0
}

/// Whether the environment holds `name` at all, empty or not.
fn is_set(name: str) -> bool {
    let _ = env_get(name) else |e| match e {
        missing => { return false },
        utf8 => { return true },
    }
    true
}

/// `--time-style=WORD` (and `TIME_STYLE`): the four named styles.
/// `posix-` in front means "only outside the POSIX locale", and this
/// program lists in the C locale alone, so such a word is the locale's
/// style whatever follows (measured: `posix-` by itself is accepted).
/// `+FORMAT` is refused by name; any other word is GNU's diagnostic,
/// whose list is its own shape and not argmatch's.
fn take_time_style(mut o: Opts, word: str) -> int {
    let w = word
    if w.starts_with("posix-") {
        o.time_style = style_locale()
        return 0
    }
    if w.starts_with("+") {
        if o.refused.len == 0 {
            o.refused = "--time-style=+FORMAT is not written yet"
        }
        return 0
    }
    let m = bore.argmatch(w, style_words(), style_meanings())
    if m < 0 {
        let what = if m == -2 then "ambiguous" else "invalid"
        eprint(
            "ls: {what} argument {bore.quote(word)} for 'time style'\nValid arguments are:\n  - [posix-]full-iso\n  - [posix-]long-iso\n  - [posix-]iso\n  - [posix-]locale\n  - +FORMAT (e.g., +%H:%M) for a 'date'-style format\nTry 'ls --help' for more information.",
        )
        return 2
    }
    o.time_style = m
    0
}

/// How an option key reads on a command line, for a refusal.
fn shown_option(k: str) -> str {
    if k.len == 1 {
        return "-{k}"
    }
    "--{k}"
}

/// A `-w`/`-T` value: a decimal count, as many digits as given (a
/// count too large for an `int` is no limit, as GNU's is). -1 for a
/// value GNU refuses.
fn line_width(v: str) -> int {
    if v.len == 0 {
        return -1
    }
    for b in v.bytes() {
        let n = b as int
        if n < 48 || n > 57 {
            return -1
        }
    }
    v.to_int() else 0
}

/// The line width `-C`, `-x` and `-m` use without `-w`: `COLUMNS` when
/// it is a count (0 is no limit), else 80, warning about a bad one as
/// GNU does — only when a format that uses the width asked.
fn columns() -> int {
    let c = env_get("COLUMNS") else |_| {
        return 80
    }
    if c.len == 0 {
        return 80
    }
    let w = line_width(c)
    if w < 0 {
        bore.warn("ls", "ignoring invalid width in environment variable COLUMNS: {bore.quote(c)}")
        return 80
    }
    w
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------- the listing

/// List every operand, files first, then the directories; answer the
/// status. `failed` comes back true when a write failed: the listing
/// goes on (GNU reports every operand it cannot reach, and the write
/// error once, at the end) but writes nothing more.
fn list_operands(
    mut out: bore.Out,
    o: Opts,
    cx: Ctx,
    operands: List[str],
    mut failed: bool,
) -> int {
    var status = 0
    var files = List[Entry]()
    var dirs = List[Entry]()
    for name in operands {
        var e = entry(name, "", -1)
        let s = stat_operand(mut e, o)
        if s > status {
            status = s
        }
        if !e.ok {
            bore.warn("ls", "cannot access {quote_always(name)}: {bore.reason_of(e.code)}")
            status = 2
        } else if e.kind == bore.kind_dir() && !o.directory {
            (mut dirs).push(e)
        } else {
            (mut files).push(e)
        }
    }
    let headers = operands.len > 1 || o.recursive
    var printed = false
    if files.len > 0 {
        let file_order = sorted(files, o)
        var bytes = List[byte]()
        // Measured: the columns of the files' listing are as wide as
        // the widest OPERAND needs, a directory among them included,
        // though its row is not printed here (`ls -l a .`).
        let s = print_entries(mut bytes, files, file_order, o, cx, false, dirs)
        if s > status {
            status = s
        }
        bore.write_all(mut out, bytes) else |_| {
            failed = true
        }
        printed = true
    }
    // What is buffered goes out before the first directory, which each
    // write straight out of their own region.
    bore.flush(mut out) else |_| {
        failed = true
    }
    let dir_order = sorted(dirs, o)
    let ancestors = List[int]()
    for at in dir_order {
        let s = list_directory(
            out,
            o,
            cx,
            dirs[at].name,
            headers,
            mut printed,
            true,
            ancestors,
            mut failed,
        )
        if s > status {
            status = s
        }
    }
    status
}

/// List the directory `path`, and with `-R` everything under it, each
/// directory in a region of its own: what one listing allocates dies
/// with it, so `-R` holds the directories it is inside and no more.
/// `printed` says whether anything has been written yet, which decides
/// the blank line before a header; `operand` says whether a failure to
/// open it is an operand's (status 2) or a subdirectory's (1);
/// `ancestors` is the device and inode of every directory this walk is
/// inside, in pairs.
fn list_directory(
    out: bore.Out,
    o: Opts,
    cx: Ctx,
    path: str,
    headers: bool,
    mut printed: bool,
    operand: bool,
    ancestors: List[int],
    mut failed: bool,
) -> int {
    var status = 0
    region listing {
        status = list_here(out, o, cx, path, headers, mut printed, operand, ancestors, mut failed)
    }
    status
}

fn list_here(
    out: bore.Out,
    o: Opts,
    cx: Ctx,
    path: str,
    headers: bool,
    mut printed: bool,
    operand: bool,
    ancestors: List[int],
    mut failed: bool,
) -> int {
    var entries = List[Entry]()
    var status = 0
    // A walk remembers the directories it is inside by device and
    // inode, and does not list one twice: under -L a link can lead back
    // up (measured: GNU says so as it would open it, status 2).
    var mine = copy ancestors
    if o.recursive {
        let here = fs_stat(path) else |_| {
            List[int]()
        }
        if here.len == 20 {
            if inside(here, ancestors) {
                bore.warn("ls", "{bore.quote_name(path)}: not listing already-listed directory")
                return 2
            }
            (mut mine).push(bore.rec_dev(here))
            (mut mine).push(bore.rec_ino(here))
        }
    }
    let opened = read_directory(mut entries, path, o, mut status)
    if !opened {
        return if operand then 2 else 1
    }
    var bytes = List[byte]()
    if headers {
        if printed {
            (mut bytes).push(10 as byte)
        }
        push_text(mut bytes, label(path, o))
        push_text(mut bytes, ":\n")
    }
    printed = true
    let s0 = stat_all(mut entries, o)
    if s0 > status {
        status = s0
    }
    let entry_order = sorted(entries, o)
    let nothing = List[Entry]()
    let s = print_entries(mut bytes, entries, entry_order, o, cx, true, nothing)
    if s > status {
        status = s
    }
    if !failed && bytes.len > 0 {
        bore.write_direct(out, bytes) else |_| {
            failed = true
        }
    }
    if !o.recursive {
        return status
    }
    for at in entry_order {
        if entries[at].name == "." || entries[at].name == ".." {
            continue
        }
        if entries[at].kind != bore.kind_dir() {
            continue
        }
        let sub = descended(path, entries[at].name)
        let s2 = list_directory(out, o, cx, sub, true, mut printed, false, mine, mut failed)
        if s2 > status {
            status = s2
        }
    }
    status
}

/// Read the names of `path` into `entries`, filtered by `-a`/`-A`.
/// Answers false, having said why, when the directory cannot be opened.
///
/// Two routes. A listing that needs nothing but names, sorted, takes
/// `fs_read_dir`'s sorted `str`s. Anything that asks about an entry —
/// its type, its record — or wants the directory's own order takes
/// `fs_read_dir_entries` (wolf 0.2.27): the host's order, and each
/// entry's type without a stat, which is also the order GNU reports the
/// entries it cannot reach in.
fn read_directory(mut entries: List[Entry], path: str, o: Opts, mut status: int) -> bool {
    if o.all == 2 {
        (mut entries).push(entry(".", path, bore.kind_dir()))
        (mut entries).push(entry("..", path, bore.kind_dir()))
    }
    if o.sort != unsorted() && !needs_kind(o) && !needs_record(o) {
        var plain = true
        let names = fs_read_dir(path) else |e| match e {
            denied => {
                bore.warn("ls", "cannot open directory {quote_always(path)}: {bore.io_error()}")
                return false
            },
            not_found => {
                bore.warn("ls", "cannot open directory {quote_always(path)}: {bore.io_error()}")
                return false
            },
            io => {
                bore.warn("ls", "cannot open directory {quote_always(path)}: {bore.io_error()}")
                return false
            },
            utf8 => {
                plain = false
                List[str]()
            },
        }
        if plain {
            for n in names {
                if o.all == 0 && n.starts_with(".") {
                    continue
                }
                (mut entries).push(entry(n, path, -1))
            }
            return true
        }
    }
    let raw = fs_read_dir_entries(path) else |_| {
        bore.warn("ls", "cannot open directory {quote_always(path)}: {bore.io_error()}")
        return false
    }
    for r in raw {
        if o.all == 0 && r.len > 1 && r[1] as int == 46 {
            continue
        }
        let n = str_from_utf8(r[1..]) else |_| {
            bore.warn(
                "ls",
                "{quote_always(path)}: a name here is not UTF-8, which no path call can name yet (wolf-lang#687)",
            )
            status = 1
            continue
        }
        (mut entries).push(entry(n, path, r[0] as int))
    }
    true
}

fn push_text(mut bytes: List[byte], s: str) {
    for b in s.bytes() {
        (mut bytes).push(b)
    }
}

/// `k` spaces.
fn push_spaces(mut bytes: List[byte], k: int) {
    var n = k
    while n > 0 {
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        n -= 1
    }
}

/// `s`, right-aligned in `width` columns.
fn push_right(mut bytes: List[byte], s: str, width: int) {
    push_spaces(mut bytes, width - s.len)
    push_text(mut bytes, s)
}

/// `s`, left-aligned in `width` columns.
fn push_left(mut bytes: List[byte], s: str, width: int) {
    push_text(mut bytes, s)
    push_spaces(mut bytes, width - s.len)
}

/// The path of `name` inside `dir` as a stat needs it: GNU names an
/// entry of `.` by its bare name in a diagnostic.
fn joined(dir: str, name: str) -> str {
    if dir == "." {
        return name
    }
    descended(dir, name)
}

/// The path of `name` inside `dir` as `-R` prints it: `./sub`, `d/sub`,
/// and `d/sub` again for an operand written `d/`.
fn descended(dir: str, name: str) -> str {
    if dir.ends_with("/") {
        return dir + name
    }
    "{dir}/{name}"
}

/// Whether the directory `rec` describes is one this walk is already
/// inside: the same device and inode as an ancestor.
fn inside(rec: List[int], ancestors: List[int]) -> bool {
    var k = 0
    while k + 1 < ancestors.len {
        if ancestors[k] == bore.rec_dev(rec) && ancestors[k + 1] == bore.rec_ino(rec) {
            return true
        }
        k += 2
    }
    false
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ the stats

/// The record of `path`, following a final link or not; an empty list
/// when the host refuses.
fn record(path: str, follow: bool) -> List[int] {
    if follow {
        let followed = fs_stat(path) else |_| {
            List[int]()
        }
        return followed
    }
    let own = fs_lstat(path) else |_| {
        List[int]()
    }
    own
}

/// Read `path`'s record INTO `e` (the list is allocated beside the
/// entry that keeps it), following a final link or not. False, with the
/// host's reason in `e.code`, when the host refuses.
fn load(mut e: Entry, path: str, follow: bool) -> bool {
    if follow {
        e.rec = fs_stat(path) else |_| {
            e.code = bore.host_code()
            List[int]()
        }
    } else {
        e.rec = fs_lstat(path) else |_| {
            e.code = bore.host_code()
            List[int]()
        }
    }
    if e.rec.len != 20 {
        return false
    }
    e.ok = true
    e.typed = true
    e.kind = bore.rec_kind(e.rec)
    true
}

/// What an operand is: `e.ok` false, and the host's reason in `e.code`,
/// when nothing reaches it. `-H` and `-L` follow a command-line link (a
/// dangling one is then unreachable, as GNU has it); by default only a
/// link to a DIRECTORY is followed, and with `-l`, `-d` or `-F` none is.
/// Answers 1 when it is a link whose target could not be read.
fn stat_operand(mut e: Entry, o: Opts) -> int {
    e.known = true
    let follow = o.deref == follow_always() || o.deref == follow_operands()
    if !load(mut e, e.name, follow) {
        return 0
    }
    if !follow && o.deref == follow_dir_operands() && e.kind == bore.kind_link() {
        let to = record(e.name, true)
        if to.len == 20 && bore.rec_kind(to) == bore.kind_dir() {
            if load(mut e, e.name, true) {
                return 0
            }
        }
    }
    link_facts(mut e, o)
}

/// When `e` is a link, what the listing needs to know about where it
/// points: its target's text for the long format, and what it resolves
/// to where that decides a mark or the grouping. Answers 1, having said
/// why, when the link cannot be read: its row then has no arrow.
fn link_facts(mut e: Entry, o: Opts) -> int {
    if e.kind != bore.kind_link() {
        return 0
    }
    let path = path_of(e)
    var status = 0
    if o.format == long_format() {
        e.arrow = true
        let bytes = fs_read_link(path) else |_| {
            bore.warn("ls", "cannot read symbolic link {quote_always(path)}: {bore.io_error()}")
            e.arrow = false
            status = 1
            List[byte]()
        }
        e.target = text_of(bytes)
    }
    // Where the link leads decides its target's mark in the long format
    // and whether --group-directories-first counts it as a directory.
    let marks = o.format == long_format() &&
        (o.indicator == classify() || o.indicator == file_type())
    if marks || o.group_dirs {
        let to = record(path, true)
        if to.len == 20 {
            e.to_kind = bore.rec_kind(to)
            e.to_mode = bore.rec_mode(to)
        }
    }
    status
}

/// A link's target as text: its bytes when they are UTF-8, else with
/// every byte outside ASCII shown as `?` (a `str` cannot hold them; the
/// byte-path lane ruling #60 names will).
fn text_of(bytes: List[byte]) -> str {
    str_from_utf8(bytes) else |_| {
        var plain = List[byte]()
        for b in bytes {
            if b as int < 128 {
                (mut plain).push(b)
            } else {
                (mut plain).push(63 as byte)
            }
        }
        str_from_utf8(plain) else "?"
    }
}

/// Read the record of an entry a directory listed, when the listing
/// needs one. Answers 1, having said why, when the host refuses: the
/// entry is then printed from what the directory said of it, as GNU
/// prints it. Under `-L` a link that cannot be followed is such an
/// entry.
fn stat_listed(mut e: Entry, o: Opts) -> int {
    e.known = true
    // A listing that prints no record asks the host only what the
    // directory's own word for the entry leaves open, and GNU does the
    // same (`ls -F` of a directory that cannot be searched complains
    // about its files and not its subdirectories): whether a regular
    // file can be executed (-F), where a link leads (-L), and the type
    // of an entry the directory did not type.
    let record_wanted = needs_record(o)
    if !record_wanted && e.typed {
        let exec = o.indicator == classify() && e.kind == bore.kind_file()
        let lead = o.deref == follow_always() && e.kind == bore.kind_link()
        if !exec && !lead {
            return 0
        }
    }
    let path = path_of(e)
    let follow = o.deref == follow_always()
    let was_typed = e.typed
    let dirent_kind = e.kind
    if !load(mut e, path, follow) {
        bore.warn("ls", "cannot access {quote_always(path)}: {bore.reason_of(e.code)}")
        e.kind = dirent_kind
        e.typed = was_typed
        if follow && !was_typed {
            // What the link itself is, for the row's first character.
            let own = record(path, false)
            if own.len == 20 {
                e.kind = bore.rec_kind(own)
                e.typed = true
            }
        }
        return 1
    }
    if record_wanted {
        return link_facts(mut e, o)
    }
    0
}

/// Whether listing `o`'s way must know what kind each entry is: to put
/// directories first, to mark them, or to descend into them.
fn needs_kind(o: Opts) -> bool {
    o.group_dirs || o.indicator != no_indicator() || o.recursive
}

/// Whether listing `o`'s way needs every entry's stat record: to print
/// it, to sort by it, or to see through a link for
/// `--group-directories-first`.
fn needs_record(o: Opts) -> bool {
    if o.format == long_format() || o.blocks || o.inode || o.group_dirs {
        return true
    }
    o.sort == by_size() || o.sort == by_time()
}

/// Whether `e` counts as a directory for `--group-directories-first`:
/// one, or a link that resolves to one.
fn is_dir_like(e: Entry) -> bool {
    e.kind == bore.kind_dir() || e.kind == bore.kind_link() && e.to_kind == bore.kind_dir()
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------- sorting

/// The order to print `es` in.
fn sorted(es: List[Entry], o: Opts) -> List[int] {
    let n = es.len
    var one = List[int]()
    var two = List[int]()
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        (mut one).push(i)
        (mut two).push(i)
        i += 1
    }
    if o.sort == unsorted() {
        return one
    }
    // A merge sort between two index lists made once, so that sorting
    // n names allocates 2n words however many passes it takes.
    var run = 1
    var in_one = true
    while run < n {
        if in_one {
            merge_pass(es, o, one, mut two, run)
        } else {
            merge_pass(es, o, two, mut one, run)
        }
        in_one = !in_one
        run *= 2
    }
    if in_one {
        return one
    }
    two
}

/// Stat every entry of a directory's listing that the listing needs
/// stat'ed, in the directory's own order. Answers 1 when the host
/// refused one.
fn stat_all(mut es: List[Entry], o: Opts) -> int {
    var status = 0
    if !needs_record(o) && !needs_kind(o) {
        return 0
    }
    var k = 0
    while k < es.len {
        if !es[k].known {
            let s = stat_listed(mut es[k], o)
            if s > status {
                status = s
            }
        }
        k += 1
    }
    status
}

/// One pass of the merge: every pair of runs of `run` in `src`, merged
/// into `dst`. Stable, so equal entries keep their order.
fn merge_pass(es: List[Entry], o: Opts, src: List[int], mut dst: List[int], run: int) {
    let n = src.len
    var at = 0
    var lo = 0
    while lo < n {
        let mid = if lo + run < n then lo + run else n
        let hi = if lo + 2 * run < n then lo + 2 * run else n
        var a = lo
        var b = mid
        while a < mid && b < hi {
            if compare(es, o, src[b], src[a]) < 0 {
                dst[at] = src[b]
                b += 1
            } else {
                dst[at] = src[a]
                a += 1
            }
            at += 1
        }
        while a < mid {
            dst[at] = src[a]
            a += 1
            at += 1
        }
        while b < hi {
            dst[at] = src[b]
            b += 1
            at += 1
        }
        lo = hi
    }
}

/// The size `-S` sorts by; an entry nothing reached sorts as empty.
fn size_of_entry(e: Entry) -> int {
    if e.ok {
        return bore.rec_size(e.rec)
    }
    0
}

/// Negative when entry `i` lists before entry `j`.
fn compare(es: List[Entry], o: Opts, i: int, j: int) -> int {
    if o.group_dirs {
        let di = is_dir_like(es[i])
        let dj = is_dir_like(es[j])
        if di && !dj {
            return -1
        }
        if dj && !di {
            return 1
        }
    }
    var c = 0
    if o.sort == by_size() {
        c = against(size_of_entry(es[j]), size_of_entry(es[i]))
    } else if o.sort == by_time() {
        if es[i].ok && es[j].ok {
            c = against(
                bore.rec_secs(es[j].rec, o.time_kind),
                bore.rec_secs(es[i].rec, o.time_kind),
            )
            if c == 0 {
                c = against(
                    bore.rec_nanos(es[j].rec, o.time_kind),
                    bore.rec_nanos(es[i].rec, o.time_kind),
                )
            }
        } else if es[i].ok {
            c = -1
        } else if es[j].ok {
            c = 1
        }
    } else if o.sort == by_extension() {
        c = text_order(extension(es[i].name), extension(es[j].name))
    }
    if c == 0 {
        c = text_order(es[i].name, es[j].name)
    }
    if o.reverse {
        return -c
    }
    c
}

fn against(a: int, b: int) -> int {
    if a < b {
        return -1
    }
    if a > b {
        return 1
    }
    0
}

/// Byte order, which is the C locale's (GNU's collation in any other
/// locale is not written: the oracle runs under `LC_ALL=C`).
fn text_order(a: str, b: str) -> int {
    if a < b {
        return -1
    }
    if a > b {
        return 1
    }
    0
}

/// What `-X` sorts by: the text after the last `.`, or nothing.
fn extension(name: str) -> str {
    let dot = name.rfind(".") else -1
    if dot < 0 {
        return ""
    }
    name[dot + 1..]
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- printing

/// The decimal digits of `n` (an id, a count or a size: never negative
/// in a record the host answered, but an inode or a device is the
/// host's unsigned word and may be).
fn count_text(n: int) -> str {
    if n >= 0 {
        return "{n}"
    }
    bore.u64_text(n as wrapping[u64])
}

/// An entry's allocated blocks as `-s` and `total` print them: in
/// 1024-byte units (512 under `POSIXLY_CORRECT` without `-k`), rounded
/// up, or in `-h`/`--si` units.
fn blocks_text(blocks: int, o: Opts, cx: Ctx) -> str {
    if o.human == 1 {
        return bore.human(blocks * 512, false)
    }
    if o.human == 2 {
        return bore.human(blocks * 512, true)
    }
    if cx.posix && !o.kibi {
        return "{blocks}"
    }
    "{(blocks + 1) / 2}"
}

/// The text before an entry's name in a listing that is not long: its
/// inode and its blocks, each right-aligned in the listing's widest.
fn prefix(e: Entry, o: Opts, cx: Ctx, ino_w: int, blk_w: int) -> str {
    var bytes = List[byte]()
    if o.inode {
        push_right(mut bytes, if e.ok then count_text(bore.rec_ino(e.rec)) else "?", ino_w)
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
    }
    if o.blocks {
        push_right(
            mut bytes,
            if e.ok then blocks_text(bore.rec_blocks(e.rec), o, cx) else "?",
            blk_w,
        )
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
    }
    str_from_utf8(bytes) else ""
}

/// One listing (the files among the operands, or one directory) in the
/// chosen format, onto `bytes`. `total` says it is a directory's, which
/// a long or `-s` listing heads with the blocks it holds; `also` holds
/// entries that are not printed here but whose columns count (the
/// directories among the operands). Answers 0.
fn print_entries(
    mut bytes: List[byte],
    es: List[Entry],
    order: List[int],
    o: Opts,
    cx: Ctx,
    total: bool,
    also: List[Entry],
) -> int {
    var ino_w = 0
    var blk_w = 0
    var sum: int = 0
    for d in also {
        if !d.ok {
            continue
        }
        if o.inode {
            let w = count_text(bore.rec_ino(d.rec)).len
            if w > ino_w {
                ino_w = w
            }
        }
        if o.blocks {
            let w = blocks_text(bore.rec_blocks(d.rec), o, cx).len
            if w > blk_w {
                blk_w = w
            }
        }
    }
    if o.inode || o.blocks || total && o.format == long_format() {
        for at in order {
            if !es[at].ok {
                if ino_w < 1 {
                    ino_w = 1
                }
                if blk_w < 1 {
                    blk_w = 1
                }
                continue
            }
            if o.inode {
                let w = count_text(bore.rec_ino(es[at].rec)).len
                if w > ino_w {
                    ino_w = w
                }
            }
            if o.blocks {
                let w = blocks_text(bore.rec_blocks(es[at].rec), o, cx).len
                if w > blk_w {
                    blk_w = w
                }
            }
            sum += bore.rec_blocks(es[at].rec)
        }
    }
    if total && (o.format == long_format() || o.blocks) {
        push_text(mut bytes, "total {blocks_text(sum, o, cx)}\n")
    }
    if o.format == long_format() {
        print_long(mut bytes, es, order, o, cx, ino_w, blk_w, also)
        return 0
    }
    var items = List[str]()
    var widths = List[int]()
    // In columns, when any name in the listing is quoted, every name that
    // is not gains one leading space, so the names line up past the
    // quote (measured, 9.11 on a terminal: `'x y'   z`); `-1` and `-m`
    // do not pad.
    var pad = false
    if o.quote && (o.format == vertical() || o.format == across()) {
        for at in order {
            if bore.quote_name(es[at].name) != es[at].name {
                pad = true
            }
        }
    }
    for at in order {
        var text = label(es[at].name, o)
        var w = label_width(es[at].name, o)
        if pad && text == es[at].name {
            text = " {text}"
            w += 1
        }
        let mark = indicator(es[at], o)
        if mark.len > 0 {
            text += mark
            w += 1
        }
        if o.inode || o.blocks {
            let before = prefix(es[at], o, cx, ino_w, blk_w)
            text = before + text
            w += before.len
        }
        (mut items).push(text)
        (mut widths).push(w)
    }
    if o.format == vertical() || o.format == across() {
        for b in bore.grid(items, widths, o.width, o.format == across(), o.tab) {
            (mut bytes).push(b)
        }
    } else if o.format == with_commas() {
        for b in bore.commas(items, widths, o.width) {
            (mut bytes).push(b)
        }
    } else {
        for t in items {
            push_text(mut bytes, t)
            (mut bytes).push(10 as byte)
        }
    }
    0
}

/// The owner column's text: the name, or the number under `-n` and for
/// an id no account carries.
fn owner_text(rec: List[int], o: Opts, cx: Ctx) -> str {
    let uid = bore.rec_uid(rec)
    if !o.numeric {
        let name = bore.user_name(cx.names, uid)
        if name.len > 0 {
            return name
        }
    }
    count_text(uid)
}

fn group_text(rec: List[int], o: Opts, cx: Ctx) -> str {
    let gid = bore.rec_gid(rec)
    if !o.numeric {
        let name = bore.group_name(cx.names, gid)
        if name.len > 0 {
            return name
        }
    }
    count_text(gid)
}

/// Whether `rec` is a device file, whose size column holds its numbers.
fn is_device(rec: List[int]) -> bool {
    bore.rec_kind(rec) == bore.kind_char() || bore.rec_kind(rec) == bore.kind_block()
}

/// A file's size as the long format prints it: bytes, or `-h`/`--si`
/// units.
fn size_text(rec: List[int], o: Opts) -> str {
    let n = bore.rec_size(rec)
    if o.human == 1 {
        return bore.human(n, false)
    }
    if o.human == 2 {
        return bore.human(n, true)
    }
    count_text(n)
}

/// The time column: the chosen time of `rec` in the chosen style, or
/// `?` when the host keeps none (a birth time, on some filesystems).
fn time_text(rec: List[int], o: Opts, cx: Ctx) -> str {
    if !bore.rec_has_time(rec, o.time_kind) {
        return "?"
    }
    let secs = bore.rec_secs(rec, o.time_kind)
    let nanos = bore.rec_nanos(rec, o.time_kind)
    let c = bore.civil_in(cx.zone, secs)
    if o.time_style == style_full_iso() {
        return bore.full_stamp(c, nanos)
    }
    let clock = "{bore.zero_padded(c.hour, 2)}:{bore.zero_padded(c.minute, 2)}"
    if o.time_style == style_long_iso() {
        return "{bore.date_text(c)} {clock}"
    }
    // Recent: within the last six months and not in the future.
    let floor = cx.now - six_months()
    let after = secs > floor || secs == floor && nanos > cx.now_nanos
    let before = secs < cx.now || secs == cx.now && nanos <= cx.now_nanos
    let recent = after && before
    if o.time_style == style_iso() {
        if recent {
            return "{bore.zero_padded(c.month, 2)}-{bore.zero_padded(c.day, 2)} {clock}"
        }
        return "{bore.date_text(c)} "
    }
    let day = "{bore.month_name(c.month)} {bore.space_padded(c.day, 2)}"
    if recent {
        return "{day} {clock}"
    }
    "{day}  {bore.year_text(c.year)}"
}

/// How wide a `?` time is: GNU pads it to the style's usual width.
fn time_width(o: Opts) -> int {
    if o.time_style == style_full_iso() {
        return 35
    }
    if o.time_style == style_long_iso() {
        return 16
    }
    if o.time_style == style_iso() {
        return 11
    }
    12
}

/// The widths of a long listing's columns.
struct Cols {
    nlink: int,
    owner: int,
    group: int,
    size: int,
    major: int,
    minor: int,
}

/// Widen `c` to hold the record `rec`.
fn widen(mut c: Cols, rec: List[int], o: Opts, cx: Ctx) {
    let w = count_text(bore.rec_nlink(rec)).len
    if w > c.nlink {
        c.nlink = w
    }
    if o.owner {
        let ow = owner_text(rec, o, cx).len
        if ow > c.owner {
            c.owner = ow
        }
    }
    if o.group {
        let gw = group_text(rec, o, cx).len
        if gw > c.group {
            c.group = gw
        }
    }
    if is_device(rec) {
        let ma = count_text(bore.dev_major(bore.rec_rdev(rec))).len
        let mi = count_text(bore.dev_minor(bore.rec_rdev(rec))).len
        if ma > c.major {
            c.major = ma
        }
        if mi > c.minor {
            c.minor = mi
        }
    } else {
        let sw = size_text(rec, o).len
        if sw > c.size {
            c.size = sw
        }
    }
}

/// The long format: one row per entry, each column as wide as its
/// widest entry in this listing.
fn print_long(
    mut bytes: List[byte],
    es: List[Entry],
    order: List[int],
    o: Opts,
    cx: Ctx,
    ino_w: int,
    blk_w: int,
    also: List[Entry],
) {
    var cols = Cols { nlink: 1, owner: 1, group: 1, size: 1, major: 0, minor: 0 }
    for at in order {
        if es[at].ok {
            widen(mut cols, es[at].rec, o, cx)
        }
    }
    for d in also {
        if d.ok {
            widen(mut cols, d.rec, o, cx)
        }
    }
    if cols.major > 0 && cols.major + 2 + cols.minor > cols.size {
        cols.size = cols.major + 2 + cols.minor
    }
    // On a terminal, as in columns: when any name of the listing is
    // quoted, every name that is not gains a leading space (measured).
    var pad = false
    if o.quote {
        for at in order {
            if bore.quote_name(es[at].name) != es[at].name {
                pad = true
            }
        }
    }
    let nlink_w = cols.nlink
    let owner_w = cols.owner
    let group_w = cols.group
    let size_w = cols.size
    let major_w = cols.major
    let minor_w = cols.minor
    for at in order {
        if o.inode {
            push_right(
                mut bytes,
                if es[at].ok then count_text(bore.rec_ino(es[at].rec)) else "?",
                ino_w,
            )
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        }
        if o.blocks {
            push_right(
                mut bytes,
                if es[at].ok then blocks_text(bore.rec_blocks(es[at].rec), o, cx) else "?",
                blk_w,
            )
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        }
        if !es[at].ok {
            // Nothing reached it: the directory's word for its type, and
            // `?` in every column.
            push_text(mut bytes, bore.kind_letter(if es[at].kind < 0 then 2 else es[at].kind))
            push_text(mut bytes, "?????????")
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
            push_right(mut bytes, "?", nlink_w)
            if o.owner {
                (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
                push_left(mut bytes, "?", owner_w)
            }
            if o.group {
                (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
                push_left(mut bytes, "?", group_w)
            }
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
            push_right(mut bytes, "?", size_w)
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
            push_right(mut bytes, "?", time_width(o))
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
            push_name(mut bytes, es[at].name, o, pad)
            push_text(mut bytes, mark_of(es[at].kind, 0, o.indicator, true))
            (mut bytes).push(10 as byte)
            continue
        }
        push_text(mut bytes, bore.mode_text(bore.rec_kind(es[at].rec), bore.rec_mode(es[at].rec)))
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        push_right(mut bytes, count_text(bore.rec_nlink(es[at].rec)), nlink_w)
        // Measured: a NAME sits to the left of its column and a NUMBER
        // (under -n, or an id no account carries) to the right.
        if o.owner {
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
            let text = owner_text(es[at].rec, o, cx)
            if o.numeric || bore.user_name(cx.names, bore.rec_uid(es[at].rec)).len == 0 {
                push_right(mut bytes, text, owner_w)
            } else {
                push_left(mut bytes, text, owner_w)
            }
        }
        if o.group {
            (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
            let text = group_text(es[at].rec, o, cx)
            if o.numeric || bore.group_name(cx.names, bore.rec_gid(es[at].rec)).len == 0 {
                push_right(mut bytes, text, group_w)
            } else {
                push_left(mut bytes, text, group_w)
            }
        }
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        if is_device(es[at].rec) {
            push_spaces(mut bytes, size_w - (major_w + 2 + minor_w))
            push_right(mut bytes, count_text(bore.dev_major(bore.rec_rdev(es[at].rec))), major_w)
            push_text(mut bytes, ", ")
            push_right(mut bytes, count_text(bore.dev_minor(bore.rec_rdev(es[at].rec))), minor_w)
        } else {
            push_right(mut bytes, size_text(es[at].rec, o), size_w)
        }
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        push_right(mut bytes, time_text(es[at].rec, o, cx), time_width_of(es[at].rec, o))
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
        push_name(mut bytes, es[at].name, o, pad)
        if es[at].kind == bore.kind_link() && !es[at].arrow {
            // A link whose target could not be read: the name alone.
        } else if es[at].kind == bore.kind_link() {
            push_text(mut bytes, " -> ")
            push_text(mut bytes, label(es[at].target, o))
            if o.indicator == classify() || o.indicator == file_type() {
                push_text(mut bytes, mark_of(es[at].to_kind, es[at].to_mode, o.indicator, true))
            }
        } else {
            push_text(mut bytes, mark_of(es[at].kind, bore.rec_mode(es[at].rec), o.indicator, true))
        }
        (mut bytes).push(10 as byte)
    }
}

/// A row's name: as `label` prints it, after one space when the
/// listing pads its unquoted names and this is one.
fn push_name(mut bytes: List[byte], name: str, o: Opts, pad: bool) {
    let text = label(name, o)
    if pad && text == name {
        (mut bytes).push(32 as byte)
    }
    push_text(mut bytes, text)
}

/// The width to right-align a row's time in: its own length (a time
/// that printed fills its column) or, for one the host does not keep,
/// the style's width.
fn time_width_of(rec: List[int], o: Opts) -> int {
    if bore.rec_has_time(rec, o.time_kind) {
        return 0
    }
    time_width(o)
}

/// The mark after a name of this `kind` and `mode` under indicator
/// `style`: `/` after a directory (every style), and under
/// `--file-type` and `-F` `@` after a link (never in the long format,
/// where the arrow says it), `|` after a pipe, `=` after a socket; `-F`
/// alone adds `*` after a regular file anyone may execute.
fn mark_of(kind: int, mode: int, style: int, long: bool) -> str {
    if style == no_indicator() || kind < 0 {
        return ""
    }
    if kind == bore.kind_dir() {
        return "/"
    }
    if style == slash() {
        return ""
    }
    if kind == bore.kind_link() {
        return if long then "" else "@"
    }
    if kind == bore.kind_fifo() {
        return "|"
    }
    if kind == bore.kind_socket() {
        return "="
    }
    if style == classify() &&
        kind == bore.kind_file() &&
        mode % 2 + mode / 8 % 2 + mode / 64 % 2 > 0 {
        return "*"
    }
    ""
}

/// The mark after an entry's name in a format that is not long.
fn indicator(e: Entry, o: Opts) -> str {
    if !e.typed {
        return ""
    }
    mark_of(e.kind, if e.ok then bore.rec_mode(e.rec) else 0, o.indicator, false)
}

/// A name as this listing prints it: in the shell-escape style on a
/// terminal (`bore.quote_name`, quoted only when it has to be: `'with
/// space'`, `'new'$'\n''line'`), else literally, through `shown`.
fn label(name: str, o: Opts) -> str {
    if o.quote {
        return bore.quote_name(name)
    }
    shown(name, o.hide)
}

/// The columns `label` takes: the quoted form is all printable ASCII.
fn label_width(name: str, o: Opts) -> int {
    if o.quote {
        return bore.quote_name(name).len
    }
    width(name, o.hide)
}

/// A name as it is printed: as it is, or with `-q` every byte outside
/// printable ASCII as `?`.
fn shown(name: str, hide: bool) -> str {
    if !hide {
        return name
    }
    var plain = true
    for b in name.bytes() {
        if !printable(b as int) {
            plain = false
        }
    }
    if plain {
        return name
    }
    var out = List[byte]()
    for b in name.bytes() {
        if printable(b as int) {
            (mut out).push(b)
        } else {
            (mut out).push(63 as byte)
        }
    }
    str_from_utf8(out) else name
}

/// The columns a name takes, as GNU counts them in the C locale: one
/// per printable byte, and an unprintable one counts nothing printed
/// as it is, one shown as `?`.
fn width(name: str, hide: bool) -> int {
    var w = 0
    for b in name.bytes() {
        if hide || printable(b as int) {
            w += 1
        }
    }
    w
}

fn printable(n: int) -> bool {
    n >= 32 && n < 127
}

/// A name quoted as GNU quotes one it could not reach: always quoted,
/// in the style `bore.quote_name` writes.
fn quote_always(name: str) -> str {
    let q = bore.quote_name(name)
    if q == name {
        return "'{name}'"
    }
    q
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- the vocabulary

/// Every option GNU's `ls` 9.11 has, served or refused. The LONG names
/// are in the order GNU lists them when an abbreviation is ambiguous
/// (`--d` names five, measured), because `bore.parse` lists them in
/// this table's order.
fn options() -> List[bore.Opt] {
    var s = List[bore.Opt]()
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("a", "all"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("A", "almost-all"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "author"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("b", "escape"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "block-size"))
    (mut s).push(bore.optional("", "classify"))
    (mut s).push(bore.optional("", "color"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("Z", "context"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("d", "directory"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("D", "dired"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("H", "dereference-command-line"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "dereference-command-line-symlink-to-dir"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("L", "dereference"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("h", "human-readable"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("q", "hide-control-chars"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "hide"))
    (mut s).push(bore.optional("", "hyperlink"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "help"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("s", "size"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "si"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "show-control-chars"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "sort"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("T", "tabsize"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "time"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "time-style"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("i", "inode"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("B", "ignore-backups"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("I", "ignore"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "indicator-style"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "full-time"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "file-type"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "format"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("Q", "quote-name"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "quoting-style"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("n", "numeric-uid-gid"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("G", "no-group"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("r", "reverse"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("R", "recursive"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "group-directories-first"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("k", "kibibytes"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("N", "literal"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("w", "width"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "zero"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "version"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("c", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("C", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("f", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("F", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("g", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("l", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("m", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("o", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("p", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("S", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("t", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("u", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("U", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("v", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("x", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("X", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("1", ""))
    s
}

/// `--format`'s words in GNU's order; the meanings are the `format`
/// numbers above.
fn format_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("verbose")
    (mut s).push("long")
    (mut s).push("commas")
    (mut s).push("horizontal")
    (mut s).push("across")
    (mut s).push("vertical")
    (mut s).push("single-column")
    s
}

fn format_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(long_format())
    (mut s).push(long_format())
    (mut s).push(with_commas())
    (mut s).push(across())
    (mut s).push(across())
    (mut s).push(vertical())
    (mut s).push(one_per_line())
    s
}

fn sort_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("none")
    (mut s).push("size")
    (mut s).push("time")
    (mut s).push("version")
    (mut s).push("extension")
    (mut s).push("name")
    (mut s).push("width")
    s
}

fn sort_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(unsorted())
    (mut s).push(by_size())
    (mut s).push(by_time())
    (mut s).push(by_version())
    (mut s).push(by_extension())
    (mut s).push(by_name())
    (mut s).push(by_width())
    s
}

/// `--time-style`'s named words, less GNU's optional `posix-`.
fn style_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("full-iso")
    (mut s).push("long-iso")
    (mut s).push("iso")
    (mut s).push("locale")
    s
}

fn style_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(style_full_iso())
    (mut s).push(style_long_iso())
    (mut s).push(style_iso())
    (mut s).push(style_locale())
    s
}

/// `--time`'s words: 0 modification, 1 access, 2 status change, 3
/// birth, the numbers `bore/info.lu` reads a record's times by.
fn time_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("atime")
    (mut s).push("access")
    (mut s).push("use")
    (mut s).push("ctime")
    (mut s).push("status")
    (mut s).push("mtime")
    (mut s).push("modification")
    (mut s).push("birth")
    (mut s).push("creation")
    s
}

fn time_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(3)
    (mut s).push(3)
    s
}

/// The WHEN of `--color` and `--classify`: 0 always, 1 never, 2 when
/// standard output is a terminal, which it is never taken to be here.
fn when_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("always")
    (mut s).push("yes")
    (mut s).push("force")
    (mut s).push("never")
    (mut s).push("no")
    (mut s).push("none")
    (mut s).push("auto")
    (mut s).push("tty")
    (mut s).push("if-tty")
    s
}

fn when_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(2)
    s
}

fn indicator_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("none")
    (mut s).push("slash")
    (mut s).push("file-type")
    (mut s).push("classify")
    s
}

fn indicator_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(no_indicator())
    (mut s).push(slash())
    (mut s).push(file_type())
    (mut s).push(classify())
    s
}

fn help() -> str {
    """
    Usage: ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...
    List each FILE, and the contents of each directory FILE (the
    working directory when there is none), sorted by name.

    Which names:
      -a, --all                  every name, the ones starting with . too
      -A, --almost-all           every name but . and ..
      -d, --directory            the operands themselves, not what they hold
      -R, --recursive            every directory under each one as well

    How they are laid out:
      -1                         one name per line (the default here)
      -C                         in columns, filled top to bottom
      -x                         in columns, filled left to right
      -m                         on lines, separated by commas
      -l                         the long format: mode, links, owner, group,
                                 size, time, name
      -g                         -l without the owner
      -o                         -l without the group
      -n, --numeric-uid-gid      -l with owner and group as numbers
      -G, --no-group             no group column in a long listing
          --full-time            -l with the whole time, to the nanosecond
          --time-style=STYLE     full-iso, long-iso, iso or locale
          --format=WORD          long or verbose (-l), vertical (-C), across
                                 or horizontal (-x), commas (-m),
                                 single-column (-1)
      -w, --width=COLS           the line width for -C, -x and -m
                                 (0: no limit; else COLUMNS, else 80)
      -T, --tabsize=COLS         a tab stop every COLS columns (0: no tabs)

    In what order (by name, in byte order, unless):
      -S                         largest first
      -t                         newest first
      -X                         by the text after the last .
      -U                         the directory's own order
      -f                         -a and -U
          --sort=WORD            none (-U), name, size (-S), time (-t),
                                 extension (-X)
      -r, --reverse              the other way round
          --group-directories-first  directories before everything else

    Which time (to show in -l; to sort by with -t, or without -l):
      -u                         the last access
      -c                         the last change of status
          --time=WORD            atime, ctime, mtime or birth

    What is shown:
      -s, --size                 each entry's allocated blocks before it
      -i, --inode                each entry's inode number before it
      -h, --human-readable       sizes and blocks in K, M, G (powers of 1024)
          --si                   the same in powers of 1000
      -k, --kibibytes            blocks of 1024 bytes (the default, unless
                                 POSIXLY_CORRECT is set)
      -F, --classify             / after a directory, @ after a link, * after
                                 an executable, | after a pipe, = after a
                                 socket
          --file-type            the same, less the *
      -p                         / after a directory
          --indicator-style=WORD none, slash (-p), file-type, classify (-F)
      -q, --hide-control-chars   ? for every byte that is not printable
          --show-control-chars   names as they are (the default off a terminal)
      -N, --literal              no quoting (on a terminal names are quoted
                                 as a shell would read them)

    Links:
      -H, --dereference-command-line  follow a link given as an operand
      -L, --dereference          follow every link

          --help                 print this help and exit
          --version              print the version and exit

    Not here yet, and refused by name: -v, the quoting styles but -N,
    --author, --block-size, --time-style=+FORMAT, and the colour and
    filtering options. On a terminal the width is -w, COLUMNS or 80: the
    terminal's own width cannot be asked. Owners and groups are named
    from /etc/passwd and /etc/group only.

    Exit status: 0 when all went well, 1 for a minor problem (an entry
    or a subdirectory it could not read), 2 for a serious one (an
    operand it could not reach, a bad option).

    boreutils: https://github.com/wolffe-lang/boreutils
    """
}
